{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The VCF Edge at Scale PowerShell module provides streamlined deployment of VCF for edge sites, offering turnkey enablement of 1\u8211-2 node clusters with vSphere Supervisor and Supervisor services (Harbor and Argo CD). Leveraging two simple JSON input files, {\f1 infrastructure.json} and {\f1 supervisor.json}, it provides a flexible, idempotent deployment mechanism to drive different, validated edge deployment topologies. A Python-based JSON generator is provided to help create the JSON files needed to generate edge configurations with safeguards against configuration errors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Prerequisites\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b vCenter:} vCenter 9.0 with a virtual datacenter and at least one vLCM image present in the Image Catalog (per {\f1 infrastructure.json}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Hosts:} ESX 9.0 installed: management and required data networks reachable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Provisioning host:} Script execution system can route to vCenter, ESX, and proposed Supervisor management network.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b PowerShell:} 7.4 or newer ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/PowerShell/PowerShell"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
PowerShell
}}}
).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b VCF PowerCLI:} {\f1 VCF.PowerCLI} on script execution system. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://developer.broadcom.com/powercli"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
VCF.PowerCLI
}}}
\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b kubectl:} installed on script execution system {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/tools/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Kubernetes tools
}}}
.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b VCF CLI:} installed on script execution system {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://techdocs.broadcom.com/us/en/vmware-cis/vcf/vcf-9-0-and-later/9-0/building-your-cloud-applications/getting-started-with-the-tools-for-building-applications/installing-and-using-vcf-cli-v9.html"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Installing and using VCF CLI v9
}}}
.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Python} The JSON generation tool requires Python 3.9 or greater. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.python.org/downloads/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Python
}}}
\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Step 1: Installation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Option 1: PowerShell Gallery (Preferred)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Install-Module -Name VcfEdgeAtScale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Option 2: Clone from GitHub and install manually\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Open a PowerShell 7.4+ prompt (pwsh on macOS/Linux, PowerShell 7 on Windows)\line
git clone https://github.com/vmware/powershell-module-for-vcf-edge-at-scale.git\line
cd powershell-module-for-vcf-edge-at-scale/VcfEdgeAtScale\line
pwsh -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\\Install-VcfEdgeAtScaleModule.ps1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Option 3: Download latest release from GitHub and install manually\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 $tempDir = New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path (Join-Path ([System.IO.Path]::GetTempPath()) ([Guid]::NewGuid().ToString()))\line
$fullPath = Join-Path -Path $tempDir -ChildPath "VcfEdgeAtScale.zip"\line
Invoke-WebRequest -Uri "https://github.com/vmware/powershell-module-for-vcf-edge-at-scale/releases/latest/download/VcfEdgeAtScale.zip" -OutFile $fullPath\line
Expand-Archive -Path $fullPath -DestinationPath $tempDir\line
cd $tempDir\line
pwsh -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\\Install-VcfEdgeAtScaleModule.ps1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Note\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b {\f1 -ExecutionPolicy Bypass}} is required on Windows when running a script downloaded from the internet. It is accepted but has no effect on macOS and Linux, so the same command works on all platforms. The installer also calls {\f1 Unblock-File} on all installed module files to remove the Windows "mark of the web" that would otherwise block {\f1 Import-Module} even after the script completes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 PowerShell auto-imports the module on first use once it is installed to {\f1 $env:PSModulePath} \u8212- no profile changes are needed. If your {\f1 $PROFILE} contains a previous {\f1 Import-Module VcfEdgeAtScale} line or lazy-load stub from an earlier install, the installer detects it and offers to clean it up. Pass {\f1 -SkipProfileUpdate} to suppress the profile check entirely (for unattended installs).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Step 2: Initialize Settings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The initialization function performs the following steps\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Establishes a base directory for the module's logs and configuration files based on user input.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Copies templated JSON files into said base directory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Copies Service YAML files into a ServicesYaml subdirectory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Copies the JSON configuration generator tool ({\f1 veas-json-generator.py}) into place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Copies local help files into place (accessible through the module).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Creates an environment variable for the base directory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Creates a logs directory for the module's debug logs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Establishes the {\f1 $env:VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory} variable pointing to the base directory.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -Initialize\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Note\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b macOS / Linux:} After {\f1 -Initialize} completes, the variable is set for the current session and the line is automatically appended to your {\f1 $PROFILE} so new terminal sessions inherit it. No manual steps are required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What {\f1 -Initialize} places on disk:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -Initialize}} (or {\b {\f1 -Initialize -InitializeTemplatesOnly}}), the base directory contains:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\f1 infrastructure.json}} / {\b {\f1 supervisor.json}} at the base (full init only, unless you decline replacement when they already exist)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\f1 ServicesYaml/}} \u8212- {\f1 1.1.0-25100889.yml}, {\f1 argocd-deployment.yml}, {\f1 harbor-data-values-v2.14.2.yml}, {\f1 legacy-harbor-svs-v2.14.2+vmware.2-vks.1-25220498.yml}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\f1 Docs/}} \u8212- {\f1 infrastructure-config-help.json}, {\f1 supervisor-config-help.json}, {\f1 README.rtf}, {\f1 EXAMPLE.rtf}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\f1 Tools/}} \u8212- {\f1 veas-json-generator.py} + {\f1 veas-ui.html} \u8212- a stdlib-only Python 3 web UI for building and validating {\f1 infrastructure.json} and {\f1 supervisor.json} without hand-editing JSON\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\f1 Logs/}} \u8212- daily deployment logs once you run {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale}} (not created by init alone beyond the empty folder)\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Step 3: Customize JSON files\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After initialization, {\f1 infrastructure.json} and {\f1 supervisor.json} must be customized for your specific edge site locations. Two approaches work equally well \u8212- use whichever fits your workflow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Option 1: Direct JSON editing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Open {\f1 $env:VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory/infrastructure.json} and {\f1 $env:VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory/supervisor.json} in any text editor. The {\f1 Docs/} folder (placed there by {\f1 -Initialize}) contains {\f1 infrastructure-config-help.json} and {\f1 supervisor-config-help.json} with field-level descriptions. Run {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -ValidateOnly} to validate your changes before deployment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Option 2 Browser-based UI tool\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Change to your {\f1 $env:VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory} directory and run:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 python3 Tools/veas-json-generator.py [--port PORT] [--base-dir DIR] [--no-browser]\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The server always binds to {\f1 127.0.0.1} (localhost only). It has no TLS and is not designed for network exposure. To reach it from a remote machine, use SSH port-forwarding: {\f1 ssh -L 8080:localhost:8080 user@host}.\par}
