{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Startup examples:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Default: loads files from the -Initialize base dir, opens browser on port 8080\line
python3 Tools/veas-json-generator.py\line
\line
# Different port\line
python3 Tools/veas-json-generator.py --port 8081\line
\line
# Point to an explicit JSON directory (useful when managing multiple environments)\line
python3 Tools/veas-json-generator.py --base-dir /path/to/my-env\line
\line
# Headless / SSH \u8212- suppress auto-open and open the URL manually\line
python3 Tools/veas-json-generator.py --no-browser\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Debug tracing:} Set {\f1 VEAS_DEBUG=1} in your environment before starting the server to print full Python tracebacks to the terminal for unexpected server-side errors. Off by default to keep the operator's terminal clean during normal use.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # macOS / Linux\line
VEAS_DEBUG=1 python3 Tools/veas-json-generator.py\line
\line
# Windows (PowerShell)\line
$env:VEAS_DEBUG = "1"; python3 Tools/veas-json-generator.py\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Port already in use:} If the port is taken (another instance is running), the script prints the conflicting PID and suggests opening the existing tab or using a different port. To kill the existing process:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # macOS / Linux\line
lsof -ti :8080 | xargs kill\line
\line
# Windows\line
netstat -ano | findstr :8080   # note the PID, then:\line
taskkill /PID <pid> /F\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b infrastructure.json not found warning:} If {\f1 infrastructure.json} is missing from the resolved base directory, the server prints a warning at startup and the UI shows the path in the directory hint. Run {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -Initialize} first, or pass {\f1 --base-dir} to point to a directory that already contains the files.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Managing sites in the UI\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The guided JSON generator provides a multi-step wizard covering common settings, edge sites, supervisor configuration, and a live preview. The following operations are available in the {\b Edge Sites} step.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 Adding a new site\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Click {\b Add Site} to open the new-site dialog.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Enter an RFC1123-compliant site name \u8212- lowercase letters, digits, and hyphens only (e.g. {\f1 edge-site-1}). The input rejects invalid characters as you type and a validation error is shown if the format is not met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Click {\b Add} to create the site card. The new site opens expanded for editing; any existing sites are collapsed to keep the page manageable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab Fill in all required fields: ESX hosts, storage type (VMFS / vSAN-OSA / vSAN-ESA), network segments, VMkernel interfaces (for vSAN), and supervisor service configuration.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 Cloning an existing site\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Cloning is the recommended way to add a second (or additional) site that shares the same topology as an existing one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Click {\b Clone} on the source site card.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Enter an RFC1123-compliant name for the new site. A preview beneath the input shows the suffix that will be appended to auto-generated segment names.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Click {\b Clone Site} to create the copy.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b What is copied:} all common settings, storage type, NIC list, VMkernel interface definitions (service and VLAN), supervisor service toggle states, and Harbor volume sizes.\par}
