{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Important\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Save & Overwrite} replaces the files the PowerShell module reads at deployment time. Make sure the configuration is valid (no errors in the Refresh pane) before saving. The automatic backup in {\f1 Backup/} retains one timestamped copy per save operation; older backups are not pruned automatically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 Loading an existing configuration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the server starts, it loads {\f1 infrastructure.json} and {\f1 supervisor.json} from the base directory. To load files from a different location without restarting the server, use the {\b Load from Directory} option in the UI (available in the wizard header). This is useful when managing multiple environments from the same workstation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Informational: Deployment Process (per edge site)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\b Cluster creation and host addition}: Creates a new cluster in vCenter and adds the specified host(s) to the cluster. If a host is already managed by vCenter before it is added to the cluster, the module handles two sub-cases:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Host has powered-on VMs:} The module refuses to proceed. It logs each running VM\u8217's name, power state, and guest OS, and prompts {\b Y/N} (default {\b N}) to continue. Answer {\b N} (or press Enter) to abort and power off or migrate the VMs first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Host has no powered-on VMs but belongs to a different cluster:} The module always prompts the operator with a {\b Y/N} (default {\b N}) move confirmation before proceeding. After confirmation, the cleanup path depends on where vmk0 currently lives:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li1080 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b vmk0 is already on a standard vSwitch:} No VDS cleanup is required. The module confirms and moves directly to the {\f1 Add-VMHost} call. This is the common case when reclaiming a host from a freshly-built or already-cleaned cluster.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li1080 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b vmk0 is on a Distributed Virtual Switch (VDS):} The module first verifies that VDS has at least two physical NIC uplinks (required for automated management restore). If the uplink prerequisite is met and the operator confirms, the module removes all non-management VMkernel adapters, migrates vmk0 back to a standard switch, and detaches the host from every VDS before calling {\f1 Add-VMHost}. If the VDS has only one uplink, the move is blocked with an error asking you to migrate vmk0 manually in vCenter first.\sa180\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Cluster configuration:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab DRS set to Automatic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab vLCM: image selection from the vCenter Image Catalog (by name from JSON or interactive prompt); cluster compliance check and remediation run before vSAN health checks (if applicable).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab VDS and networking:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab vSphere Distributed Switch (VDS) creation with required port groups (management, vMotion, vSAN, vSAN Witness as configured) and VMkernel interfaces. Management (vmk0) remains management-only; vMotion, vSAN, and vSAN Witness use dedicated VMkernel interfaces when configured. With {\b four physical NICs} (two VDS instances, {\f1 -sw1} and {\f1 -sw2}), {\b vSAN witness traffic stays on the first VDS} ({\f1 VDS-<edgesite>-sw1}): the dedicated witness VMkernel (vmk3) and its distributed port group are created there; vMotion and vSAN VMkernels remain on the second VDS ({\f1 VDS-<edgesite>-sw2}). With two NICs, all VMkernel port groups use the single VDS.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab The management vmkernel interface will be automatically migrated from a standard vSwitch to a new vDS during the initialization process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab Physical NIC connectivity check before VDS creation; active/standby uplink teaming; MTU from config (default 9000 for vMotion/vSAN, 1500 for management and vSAN Witness).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab The {\b vSAN Witness} VMkernel interface may be configured with an optional {\b {\f1 gateway}}.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab {\b Storage}:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b VMFS}: When this storage type is selected, the largest, unformatted disk is automatically selected for as a VMFS datastore.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b vSAN OSA or vSAN ESA}: For vSAN enabled configurations, OSA or ESA, auto-disk claim is utilized. Both vSAN variants storage types require a vSAN witness. The witness can be defined at the common level or the edge site level. If both are defined, the edge site takes priority. Please take note: there are two different types of vSAN witness virtual appliances, one for ESA and one for OSA; the witness ESX version must also match the ESX host version. Throughout vSAN health checks and vLCM gate checks are performed before a supervisor deployment may begin.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 5.\tx360\tab {\b vSphere Supervisor enablement}: Unless the edge site is deployed with the {\f1 -ComputeOnly} flag, a Supervisor is automatically deployed upon successful standup of the compute and networking stack. After which, the PowerShell module will check if there are any pending supervisor updates available and apply them. Unless Argo CD and/or Harbor are disabled, it will then proceed to deploy one or both of those services.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 6.\tx360\tab {\b Argo CD instance creation}: Argo CD instance created and configured. After deployment, it prints the URL and temporary admin password for immediate use.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 7.\tx360\tab {\b Harbor instance creation}: Harbor registry created and configured from a templated data file so many edge sites stay consistent and easier to maintain. The code will also show a URL and login password post deployment.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Informational: In-Application Help\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # View infrastructure.json configuration reference (auto-detects best format)\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp\line
\line
# View supervisor.json configuration reference (auto-detects best format)\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp\line
\line
# Use list format for narrow screens\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp -Format List\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp -Format List\line
\line
# Use table format for wide screens (with separators for readability)\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp -Format Table\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp -Format Table\line
\line
# Use interactive grid view (Windows PowerShell only)\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp -Format GridView\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp -Format GridView\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Step 4: Deployment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Deployment Parameters:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 AcceptBadCheckResults} (Switch, optional) - When specified, automatically proceed when vSAN cluster health is red or when vLCM cluster compliance remediation fails (no Y/N prompts).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b PSGallery install} ({\f1 Install-Module}): prompts to run {\f1 Update-Module} automatically if a newer version is available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Manual install} (git clone / {\f1 Install-VcfEdgeAtScaleModule.ps1}): announces the newer version and shows the {\f1 git pull} + re-run installer steps; no automatic install is attempted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab If PSGallery cannot be reached (network error, proxy, air-gap), the check is silently skipped \u8212- it is always non-fatal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab For silent once-per-day auto-checks see {\f1 common.autoUpdate} in the infrastructure JSON reference table below.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 CleanUp} (String, optional) - Cleanup only; no deploy. Must be {\f1 Supervisor}, {\f1 Compute}, {\f1 All}, {\f1 ArgoCD}, or {\f1 Harbor}. {\b Supervisor} disables Supervisor only. {\b Compute} removes VDS, storage, and cluster (fails if Supervisor exists). {\b All} disables Supervisor then removes compute. {\b ArgoCD} removes only the Argo CD supervisor namespace (polls until gone). {\b Harbor} removes only the Harbor Supervisor Service (polls until gone). Confirm with a typed phrase such as {\f1 delete harbor for site1} unless {\f1 -Force} is used with {\f1 common.labenvironment} true (see module help for the exact pattern).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 ComputeOnly} (Switch, optional) - Run all pre-supervisor steps (clusters, hosts, storage, VDS, vLCM) then exit without enabling the supervisor or deploying Argo CD.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 EdgeSite} (String, optional) - Comma-delimited list of edge site names (e.g. {\f1 "site1,site2"}). Deploy only clusters whose {\f1 edgeSite} matches one of the values, in the order listed. Omit to deploy all clusters. Only comma is allowed as separator; invalid delimiters or unknown site names cause the workflow to fail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 Force} (Switch, optional) - When {\f1 common.labenvironment} is true in infrastructure JSON, bypasses the cleanup confirmation prompt when using {\f1 -CleanUp}. Has no effect otherwise; a warning is shown if used without lab.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 InfrastructureJson} (String, optional) - Path to infrastructure configuration JSON file. When omitted (or null/empty), defaults to **{\f1 Join-Path $env:VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory 'infrastructure.json'}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 LogLevel} (String, optional) - Minimum log level for console output. Valid values: {\f1 DEBUG}, {\f1 INFO}, {\f1 ADVISORY}, {\f1 WARNING}, {\f1 EXCEPTION}, {\f1 ERROR}. Default: {\f1 "INFO"}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 RollbackOnFailure} (Boolean, optional) - When {\f1 $true}: always rollback on failure (no prompt; for autonomous runs). When {\f1 $false}: never rollback; leave site in current state and continue to next site if any. When omitted: prompt (Y/N/Always). Rollback scope is automatic by failure state (see {\b Rollback behavior} below). {\b Operational guidance:} answer Y (or use {\f1 $true}) before you re-try after a failure so the site is torn back to a consistent state. Use {\f1 $false} or N only for intentional leave-as-is debugging; afterward run the appropriate {\f1 -CleanUp} for that scope and edge site (for example {\f1 -CleanUp Harbor -EdgeSite site1}) before another full deployment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 SaveHarborYaml} (Switch, optional) - When specified, the per-site Harbor data values YAML generated at runtime is moved to a {\f1 HarborYaml} subdirectory under the module directory instead of being deleted after installation. {\b Warning:} this file contains Harbor passwords and secrets in plain text. A {\f1 [WARNING]} is shown whenever this switch is used. Delete the file immediately after inspecting it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 SupervisorJson} (String, optional) - Path to supervisor configuration JSON file. When omitted (or null/empty), defaults to {\b {\f1 Join-Path $env:VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory 'supervisor.json'}}.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Rollback behavior:} Scope follows where the run failed: compute or vSAN before Supervisor \u8594 ? full compute teardown; after compute but before Argo CD \u8594 ? Supervisor disabled only; Argo CD issues \u8594 ? namespace removed, Supervisor kept; Harbor issues \u8594 ? Harbor service removed only. {\f1 -RollbackOnFailure} sets prompt, always, or never.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Re-trying after a failure:} Prefer rolling back first (interactive Y or A, or {\f1 -RollbackOnFailure $true}). Skipping rollback ({\f1 N} or {\f1 -RollbackOnFailure $false}) is for manual inspection only. After debugging, either re-run and choose rollback at the next failure prompt, or force a clean baseline with {\f1 -CleanUp} (same scopes as {\b Cleanup} above): for example {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp ArgoCD -EdgeSite site1} if only the Argo CD namespace should be removed, {\f1 -CleanUp Harbor} if only Harbor should be removed, {\f1 -CleanUp Supervisor} to disable the supervisor, {\f1 -CleanUp Compute} to remove compute (fails if the supervisor is still enabled), or {\f1 -CleanUp All} for supervisor then compute. Then fix your JSON or YAML and deploy again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Examples:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Default edge deployment (All edge sites defined in JSON files)\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale\line
\line
# Deployment with custom configuration files\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale `\line
    -InfrastructureJson "infrastructure-test.json" `\line
    -SupervisorJson "prod-supervisor-test.json"\line
\line
# Deployment with DEBUG logging for troubleshooting\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -LogLevel DEBUG\line
\line
# Deploy only a specific edge site (targeted deployments or troubleshooting)\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# Deploy multiple edge sites in order (comma-delimited; only comma is allowed as separator)\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -EdgeSite "site1,site2"\line
\line
# Deploy specific edge site(s) with custom configuration files\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale `\line
    -EdgeSite "site2" `\line
    -InfrastructureJson "infrastructure-test.json" `\line
    -SupervisorJson "supervisor-test.json"\line
\line
# Autonomous run: always rollback on failure without prompting\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -RollbackOnFailure $true\line
\line
# Never rollback on failure; leave site in current state and continue to next site\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -RollbackOnFailure $false\line
\line
# Compute-only: prepare clusters, hosts, storage, VDS, and vLCM; exit without enabling supervisor\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -ComputeOnly\line
\line
# Cleanup supervisor only for site1 (compute remains); you must type "delete supervisor for site1" to confirm\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp Supervisor -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# Cleanup Argo CD namespace only for site1 (supervisor and compute remain); confirm with "delete argocd for site1"\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp ArgoCD -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# Cleanup Harbor Supervisor Service only for site1 (supervisor, Argo CD, and compute remain); confirm with "delete harbor for site1"\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp Harbor -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# Cleanup compute only for site1 (fails if supervisor is deployed); confirm with "delete compute for site1"\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp Compute -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# Cleanup all (supervisor then compute) for all sites; confirm per site with "delete all for <edgeSite>"\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp All\line
\line
# Cleanup all with -Force when common.labenvironment is true in infrastructure.json (bypasses confirmation)\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp All -Force\line
\line
# Accept bad vSAN health or vLCM compliance (proceed without Y/N prompts)\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -AcceptBadCheckResults\line
\line
# Validate JSON and YAML only (no deployment) using files under VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -ValidateOnly\line
\line
# Or validate explicit paths (VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory must still be set; these paths override the default JSON filenames only)\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -ValidateOnly -InfrastructureJson "infrastructure-test.json" -SupervisorJson "supervisor-test.json"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Informational: Operational modes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Most commands may be issued through {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale}}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Deployment} ({\f1 -EdgeSite}) : Specify one or more EdgeSites\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Validate only} ({\f1 -ValidateOnly}): JSON checks (including network segments) and YAML path checks only; no vCenter session and no deployment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Compute only} ({\f1 -ComputeOnly}): Cluster, hosts, storage, VDS, and vLCM through remediation; stops before Supervisor, Argo CD, and Harbor. JSON/YAML validation is limited to compute paths (supervisor.json, Argo/Harbor YAML, and {\f1 common.contextName} are not required).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Cleanup} ({\f1 -CleanUp}): Removes the chosen scope without deploying. Values: {\f1 Supervisor}, {\f1 Compute}, {\f1 All}, {\f1 ArgoCD}, {\f1 Harbor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Supervisor:} Disable Supervisor; compute stays.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Compute:} Remove VDS, storage, and cluster; fails if Supervisor is still enabled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b All:} Disable Supervisor, then remove compute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b ArgoCD:} Remove the Argo CD supervisor namespace for that cluster; script waits until it is gone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Harbor:} Remove the Harbor Supervisor Service from the supervisor; script waits until it is gone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab Typed confirmation is required unless {\f1 -Force} is used with {\f1 common.labenvironment} true (for example {\f1 delete harbor for site1}; use your cleanup scope and edge site name).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Rollback on failure:} Rollback follows the failure point (compute, Supervisor, Argo CD namespace, or Harbor service). {\f1 -RollbackOnFailure} selects always, never, or prompt (Y/N/Always). For a normal fix-and-retry workflow, choose rollback (Y, A, or {\f1 -RollbackOnFailure $true}) before you change configuration and run again. Use N or {\f1 -RollbackOnFailure $false} only when you deliberately keep a partial deployment open for hands-on debugging; when you are finished, run the matching {\f1 -CleanUp} scope (see {\b Cleanup} above) for that edge site so the next run does not stack on a broken half-state.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Collect logs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If you run into a problem and need to share a log bundle with Broadcom, please run the following:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CollectLogs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The module creates a zip archive under your home directory containing infrastructure.json, supervisor.json, the {\f1 Logs/} folder, and the {\f1 ServicesYaml/} folder from {\f1 $env:VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Check module version\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -Version\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Check for new releases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Note\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The module automatically checks for new versions once per day when used. The behaviour differs by install source:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b PSGallery install} ({\f1 Install-Module}): when a newer version is available, you are prompted to run {\f1 Update-Module} automatically (default Y). After a successful update both {\f1 veas-json-generator.py} and {\f1 veas-ui.html} are synced to your deployment root if their versions differ.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Manual / GitHub install} (git clone + {\f1 Install-VcfEdgeAtScaleModule.ps1}): announces the newer version and shows the manual update steps ({\f1 git pull} + re-run installer); no automatic install is attempted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab If PSGallery cannot be reached (network error, proxy, air-gap), the check is silently skipped \u8212- it is always non-fatal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Disable automatic daily checks by setting {\f1 "autoUpdate": false} in {\f1 common} of your {\f1 infrastructure.json}.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CheckForUpdates\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b EdgeSite Parameter Usage:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\f1 -EdgeSite} parameter lets you target one or more edge sites from your infrastructure.json instead of processing all clusters. Use a comma-delimited list to deploy multiple sites (e.g. {\f1 -EdgeSite "site1,site2"}). This is useful for the following:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Targeted deployments}: Deploy or redeploy specific edge sites without processing others\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Troubleshooting}: Isolate issues to specific clusters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Incremental rollouts}: Deploy a subset of sites in a chosen order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Testing}: Validate configuration for selected sites before deploying all\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b How it works:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab When {\f1 -EdgeSite} is {\b not specified}: All clusters in the {\f1 clusters[]} array are processed sequentially. The script connects to vCenter once and processes each cluster in order, maintaining the connection between clusters that share the same vCenter FQDN.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab When {\f1 -EdgeSite} {\b is specified}: Only clusters whose {\f1 edgeSite} matches one of the comma-separated values are processed, in the order you list them (e.g. {\f1 -EdgeSite "site2,site1"} deploys site2 then site1).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Important Notes:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Use {\b only a comma} to separate site names. Invalid delimiters (e.g. semicolon) cause the workflow to fail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Each value must exactly match an {\f1 edgeSite} in your infrastructure.json {\f1 clusters[]} array. If any specified site is invalid, the workflow fails with the list of valid values.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab All clusters in the same infrastructure.json share the same vCenter connection (from {\f1 common.vCenterName}), so the connection persists when processing multiple clusters.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Displays a reference table for configuring the {\f1 infrastructure.json} file (Key, Required, Notes).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Parameters:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 Format} (String, optional) - Output format. Valid values: {\f1 Auto} (default), {\f1 List}, {\f1 Table}, {\f1 GridView}. Default: {\f1 Auto}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 Filter} (String, optional) - Filters configuration elements by Element Name using wildcard matching. The filter is automatically wrapped with wildcards (*) on both sides.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Examples:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Display infrastructure.json configuration reference (auto-detects best format)\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp\line
\line
# Use list format for narrow screens\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp -Format List\line
\line
# Use table format for wide screens (with separators)\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp -Format Table\line
\line
# Use interactive grid view (Windows PowerShell only)\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp -Format GridView\line
\line
# Filter to show only argoCD related elements\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp -Filter argoCD\line
\line
# Combine filter with format\line
Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp -Filter storagePolicy -Format List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Format Options:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Auto}: Automatically selects the best format based on terminal width\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b List}: Vertical layout, ideal for narrow screens (40-50+ characters)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Table}: Table format with horizontal separators, ideal for wide screens (120+ characters)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b GridView}: Interactive window with sorting/filtering (Windows PowerShell only)\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The output includes detailed information about:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab vCenter and ESX host configuration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Cluster and datacenter settings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Storage policy and datastore configuration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Virtual Distributed Switch (VDS) settings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Port group and network configuration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Argo CD operator and deployment settings\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Displays a reference table for configuring the {\f1 supervisor.json} file (Key, Required, Notes).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Parameters:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 Format} (String, optional) - Output format. Valid values: {\f1 Auto} (default), {\f1 List}, {\f1 Table}, {\f1 GridView}. Default: {\f1 Auto}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 Filter} (String, optional) - Filters configuration elements by Element Name using wildcard matching. The filter is automatically wrapped with wildcards (*) on both sides.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Examples:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Display supervisor.json configuration reference (auto-detects best format)\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp\line
\line
# Use list format for narrow screens\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp -Format List\line
\line
# Use table format for wide screens (with separators)\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp -Format Table\line
\line
# Use interactive grid view (Windows PowerShell only)\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp -Format GridView\line
\line
# Filter to show only supervisor site elements\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp -Filter siteSpec\line
\line
# Filter for load balancer elements\line
Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp -Filter flb -Format List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Format Options:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Auto}: Automatically selects the best format based on terminal width\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b List}: Vertical layout, ideal for narrow screens (40-50+ characters)\par}
