{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Uninstallation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Step 1 \u8212- Remove the module files\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b PowerShell Gallery install} ({\f1 Install-Module}):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Uninstall-Module -Name VcfEdgeAtScale -AllVersions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Manual install} (git clone or GitHub release + {\f1 Install-VcfEdgeAtScaleModule.ps1}):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Locate the installed directory\line
$modulePath = (Get-Module -Name VcfEdgeAtScale -ListAvailable | Select-Object -First 1).ModuleBase\line
\line
# Unload from the current session, then delete\line
Remove-Module -Name VcfEdgeAtScale -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue\line
Remove-Item -Path $modulePath -Recurse -Force\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Step 2 \u8212- Remove the {\f1 VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory} environment variable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 During {\f1 -Initialize}, the working-directory path is persisted differently depending on the operating system. Follow the steps for your platform, then clear it from the current session.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 Windows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The variable is stored in the {\b user-level registry} ({\f1 HKCU\\Environment}). Remove it with:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Remove from the registry (affects all future sessions)\line
[System.Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable(\line
    "VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory", $null, [System.EnvironmentVariableTarget]::User)\line
\line
# Clear from the current session\line
Remove-Item Env:\\VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 You can verify the variable is gone by opening a new PowerShell session and checking:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 $env:VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory   # should be empty / $null\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 macOS and Linux\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The variable is written as a line in your PowerShell {\f1 $PROFILE} file. The line looks like:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 $env:VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory = "/Users/you/VcfEdgeAtScale"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Remove it automatically with the one-liner below, or open {\f1 $PROFILE} in a text editor and delete the line manually:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Remove the VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory line from $PROFILE\line
$profileContent = Get-Content -LiteralPath $PROFILE -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue\line
if ($profileContent) \{\line
    $cleaned = ($profileContent -split "`n" |\line
        Where-Object \{ $_ -notmatch "VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory" \}) -join "`n"\line
    Set-Content -LiteralPath $PROFILE -Value $cleaned -Encoding UTF8 -NoNewline\line
\}\line
\line
# Clear from the current session\line
Remove-Item Env:\\VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Step 3 \u8212- Remove any profile entries left by older installs (all platforms)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Earlier versions of the installer may have written an {\f1 Import-Module VcfEdgeAtScale} eager-load line or a lazy-load stub to {\f1 $PROFILE}. These are harmless once the module is removed (they produce a benign error on load), but you can clean them up:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Preview lines that mention VcfEdgeAtScale\line
(Get-Content -LiteralPath $PROFILE -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) |\line
    Where-Object \{ $_ -match "VcfEdgeAtScale" \}\line
\line
# Remove all VcfEdgeAtScale references from $PROFILE\line
$profileContent = Get-Content -LiteralPath $PROFILE -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue\line
if ($profileContent) \{\line
    $cleaned = ($profileContent -split "`n" |\line
        Where-Object \{ $_ -notmatch "VcfEdgeAtScale" \}) -join "`n"\line
    Set-Content -LiteralPath $PROFILE -Value $cleaned -Encoding UTF8 -NoNewline\line
\}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Step 4 \u8212- Remove the working directory (optional)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The working directory created by {\f1 -Initialize} (the path stored in {\f1 VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory}) contains your configuration files ({\f1 infrastructure.json}, {\f1 supervisor.json}), deployment logs, and service YAML files. It is {\b not removed automatically} \u8212- you must delete it manually if you no longer need it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Warning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This step is destructive. Back up any configuration files or logs you want to keep before proceeding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Capture the path before the variable is cleared\line
$workDir = $env:VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory\line
\line
# Confirm, then delete\line
if ($workDir -and (Test-Path $workDir)) \{\line
    Remove-Item -Path $workDir -Recurse -Force\line
\}\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Configuration Files\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Deployment is driven by two JSON inputs: {\f1 infrastructure.json} (vCenter, clusters, networking, storage, Argo CD paths) and {\f1 supervisor.json} (supervisor sizing, load balancer, and network IP ranges). Each {\f1 edgeSite} in {\f1 infrastructure.json} {\f1 clusters[]} must have a matching {\f1 edgeSite} in {\f1 supervisor.json} {\f1 siteSpec[]}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 infrastructure.json\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This file defines vCenter connection, datacenter, and shared naming prefixes; then one or more clusters, each with ESX hosts, storage type, networking segments, and Argo CD paths. Many fields are {\b optional} and have defaults so you can omit them for a minimal config.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Structure:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\f1 common}} \u8211- Settings used for all clusters (one vCenter, one datacenter, shared prefixes and content library). Prefixes and {\f1 esxUser} are optional; when omitted, defaults apply (see tables below).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\f1 clusters}} \u8211- Array of cluster objects. Each cluster is identified by {\f1 edgeSite} (must match a site in {\f1 supervisor.json}). Per-cluster you define ESX hosts, storage type, and network segments. {\b {\f1 supervisorServices}} file names, {\b {\f1 parentDirectory}}, or legacy {\b {\f1 *YamlPath}} keys may be defined here to override the common defaults.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Important behavior:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Uplinks:} There is no {\f1 numUplinks} field. The number of VDS uplinks is taken from the length of {\f1 nicList} (e.g. two NICs in {\f1 nicList} means two uplinks). {\b nicList} may be defined at {\b common} (applies to all clusters) or per {\b clusters[]} (cluster overrides common). At least one definition is mandatory: either {\f1 common.nicList} or {\f1 clusters[].nicList} for each cluster. When both are defined, the cluster value takes precedence. Effective list must have 2 or 4 NICs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Storage:} Use {\f1 storagePolicy.storageType} ({\f1 VMFS}, {\f1 vSAN-ESA}, or {\f1 vSAN-OSA}). The tag catalog name can be omitted; it defaults to {\f1 \{storageType\}-Storage-TagCatalog} (e.g. {\f1 VMFS-Storage-TagCatalog}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b vSAN witness:} {\f1 vSanWitnessVmName} may be defined at {\f1 common} (applies to all vSAN clusters) or per {\f1 clusters[]} (cluster root level only; cluster overrides common). Required for vSAN-OSA and vSAN-ESA storage types; not used for VMFS.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Cluster name override:} Set {\f1 clusters[].overrideClusterName} to give a specific vSphere cluster name to a single edge site, bypassing the {\f1 \{clusterNamePrefix\}-\{edgeSite\}} formula entirely for that cluster. The value must be 1-80 characters (alphanumeric, spaces, {\f1 _}, {\f1 +}, {\f1 -}, {\f1 ()}, {\f1 .}); JSON validation and PowerShell both enforce this. {\f1 common.clusterNamePrefix} is still used for all other clusters that do not set this key.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b vSphere HA admission (vSAN OSA/ESA only):} {\f1 haPolicy} may be defined at {\f1 common} or per {\f1 clusters[]} (cluster overrides common). Omitted means {\b {\f1 reservationBased}} (CPU and memory percentage admission; default percentage is ceiling(100 / host count) unless overridden). {\b {\f1 slotBased}} uses host failures tolerated = 1 (slot-style admission). {\b {\f1 disabled}} leaves HA on with admission control off (VM restart only, no capacity reservation). These values apply when the workflow re-applies HA after moving management to the VDS and when the vSAN alarm check remediates {\b vSphere HA host status}. {\b VMFS} clusters are not governed by this JSON key; HA re-apply after the VDS step continues to use {\b reservationBased}. When the key is present, JSON validation accepts only the three strings above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b vSAN Health \u8212- Stats Primary election:} The module {\b does not} add Stats Primary to the vSAN {\b silent-check} list (no {\f1 VsanHealthSetVsanClusterSilentChecks} for that test). When the post-witness health summary is non-green {\b only} for Stats Primary election/selection (including common {\f1 perfsvc.masterexist} / label variants on older builds), it {\b re-triggers} evaluation: ensures the vSAN performance service is enabled, runs {\b {\f1 Test-VsanClusterHealth}} when the cmdlet exists (with {\b {\f1 VMCreateTimeoutSeconds}} when supported\u8212-default 120), waits between attempts, and re-reads health up to {\b four} times (45 s between attempts by default). If it still only shows that finding, deployment {\b proceeds with a warning} so a transient leader election does not block the gate. {\f1 common.labenvironment: true} still applies {\b separate} lab-only silent checks for other tests. After a successful vSAN edge deployment, {\b {\f1 Test-VsanClusterHealth}} runs again when available. Manual remediation remains per {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/401679/stats-primary-election-alert-via-vsan-he.html"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Broadcom KB 401679
}}}
. The module does not SSH to ESX to restart services automatically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Lab environment and the vSAN HCL (important):} When {\b {\f1 common.labenvironment}} is {\b {\f1 true}}, {\f1 Set-VsanLabSilentChecksIfRequested} silences these vSAN Health checks before the post-witness evaluation: {\b {\f1 advcfgsync}}, {\b {\f1 controllerdiskmode}}, {\b {\f1 controlleronhcl}}, {\b {\f1 controllerfirmware}}, {\b {\f1 controllerdriver}}, {\b {\f1 hclhostbadstate}}. Only {\f1 advcfgsync} is a transient-sync test; the other five map to vSAN HCL / hardware-compatibility findings (storage controller on HCL, controller disk mode, firmware, driver, and host HCL DB state). Silencing changes only the vCenter {\b signal layer} (health test status and the cluster alarms derived from it); it does {\b not} change the underlying hardware state. Two consequences you should plan for:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab The same cluster may deploy cleanly in lab mode and {\b fail to enable Supervisor outside lab mode on identical hardware}, because WCP enforces cluster/host HCL conformance downstream of this module's red-alarm gate. Accepting the red vSAN alarm in non-lab mode does not fix the HCL state.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\f1 Invoke-VsanClusterAlarmCheckAndRemediate} classifies each blocking red alarm with {\b {\f1 Test-VsanTriggeredAlarmIsHclRelated}} (matches {\b HCL}, {\b hardware compatibility}, {\b hardware vSAN support}, and {\b controller firmware/driver/disk mode/on HCL}). When any blocking red alarm is HCL-related, the {\b ERROR/WARNING} banner, {\b {\f1 AcceptBadCheckResults}} message, {\b Y/N} prompt, and the {\f1 Y}-accepted log line all explicitly state that Supervisor enablement is expected to fail until the cluster is HCL-conformant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab For production-equivalent edge deployments use {\b HCL-listed} storage controllers, firmware revisions, and drivers, and keep {\b {\f1 common.labenvironment}} set to {\b {\f1 false}}. Use {\b {\f1 true}} only for nested/demo environments where HCL conformance is known not to hold and Supervisor enablement is expected to work only because WCP's nested-host path tolerates those signals; do not rely on a successful lab-mode run to predict non-lab-mode behavior.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Supervisor service YAML files:} Preferred: under {\f1 supervisorServices}, set {\b {\f1 parentDirectory}} (folder containing the YAML files) and {\b {\f1 argoCdOperatorYamlFileName}}, {\b {\f1 argoCdDeploymentYamlFileName}}, {\b {\f1 harborDataTemplateYamlFileName}}, and {\b {\f1 harborServiceYamlFileName}} (file names only). Define these at {\f1 common} and/or override per {\f1 clusters[].supervisorServices} (cluster wins per key). If a cluster omits a file name, the common value is used when resolving the combined path. {\b Legacy:} omit {\b {\f1 parentDirectory}} (and rely on {\b {\f1 \\*YamlPath}} instead) by setting {\b {\f1 argoCdOperatorYamlPath}}, {\b {\f1 argoCdDeploymentYamlPath}}, {\b {\f1 harborDataTemplateYamlPath}}, and {\b {\f1 harborServiceYamlPath}} to full or JSON-relative paths at {\f1 common} and/or per cluster (cluster overrides common per key). When {\f1 disableArgoCD} is true, Argo paths are not required. When {\f1 disableHarbor} is true, Harbor YAML paths are not required. {\f1 supervisorServices.nameSpacePrefix} defaults to {\f1 argocd} if omitted. {\f1 supervisorServices.vmClass} is optional; when omitted, the script assigns all VM classes reported by vCenter to the ArgoCD namespace.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Disabling supervisor services:} Set {\f1 supervisorServices.disableArgoCD: true} or {\f1 supervisorServices.disableHarbor: true} at {\f1 common} (affects all clusters) or per {\f1 clusters[]} (overrides common for that cluster only). Both default to {\f1 false} (enabled). When {\f1 disableArgoCD} is true, the Argo CD service, namespace, operator, and instance steps are all skipped and Argo file names do not need to be configured. When {\f1 disableHarbor} is true, Harbor service registration, data values generation, and installation are all skipped; {\f1 harborConfiguration} is not required and Harbor YAML file names do not need to be configured. {\b Service flags also affect the LB virtual server network IP minimum} (see {\f1 flbVirtualServerNetwork.flbNetworkIpAddressCount} in the supervisor.json reference): Harbor enabled requires at least 50 IPs; Argo CD only requires at least 20; neither requires at least 10.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Harbor configuration:} Each cluster needs a {\f1 harborConfiguration} block unless {\f1 disableHarbor} is true.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Hostname:} {\f1 harborConfiguration.hostname} is required (FQDN or IP that DNS accepts) unless {\b {\f1 common.labenvironment}} is {\b {\f1 true}}, Harbor is enabled, and {\b {\f1 tlsCrt}} / {\b {\f1 tlsKey}} are {\b both omitted}\u8212-then the hostname is read from the top-level {\f1 hostname:} line in the Harbor data values template ({\b {\f1 harborDataTemplateYamlFileName}} / {\b {\f1 harborDataTemplateYamlPath}}). You may still set {\b {\f1 hostname}} in JSON to override the template.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Volume sizes:} Optional {\f1 registryVolumeSize}, {\f1 jobserviceVolumeSize}, {\f1 databaseVolumeSize}, {\f1 redisVolumeSize}, {\f1 trivyVolumeSize} use {\f1 <positive integer>Gi} (for example {\f1 10Gi}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Secrets:} Optional keys map into the Harbor data values YAML. Prefix with {\f1 $env:VAR} to load at runtime so secrets stay out of JSON.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Prompts:} If {\f1 $env:} is used and the variable is unset, the script asks once during pre-flight (masked input) and caches the value for the process. Plain text in JSON is never prompted for.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b TLS:} {\b {\f1 tlsCrt}} and {\b {\f1 tlsKey}} must be defined {\b together} or {\b both omitted}; defining only one is an error. When using your own PEMs, either set {\b {\f1 harborConfiguration.parentDirectory}} and use {\b {\f1 tlsCrt}} / {\b {\f1 tlsKey}} as {\b file names} under that directory (both required together), with optional {\b {\f1 caCrt}} as a file name when both TLS files are set, {\b or} omit {\b {\f1 parentDirectory}} and set {\b {\f1 tlsCrt}}, {\b {\f1 tlsKey}}, and optional {\b {\f1 caCrt}} to full or infrastructure-relative PEM paths (legacy). Paths are validated before deploy. When {\b {\f1 common.labenvironment}} is {\b {\f1 true}} and {\b both} {\b {\f1 tlsCrt}} and {\b {\f1 tlsKey}} are omitted, the module generates a {\b self-signed} RSA certificate (via .NET, works on Windows and macOS/Linux), injects it into the Harbor data values YAML, and uses the same PEM for Supervisor container-registry trust; post-install {\b Harbor connection details} note that TLS is self-signed for lab. Set {\b {\f1 common.preserveAutoGeneratedKeyCertPair}} to {\b {\f1 true}} to save the generated key and certificate to {\b {\f1 $env:VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory/HarborKeyCerts/<edgeSite>/}} (as {\b {\f1 <edgeSite>.key}} / {\b {\f1 <edgeSite>.crt}}) after a successful deployment; on non-Windows the private key is restricted to owner-read-only ({\f1 chmod 0600}). This has no effect when {\f1 tlsCrt}/{\f1 tlsKey} are supplied or when {\f1 labenvironment} is {\f1 false}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Service YAML:} Download the Harbor Supervisor Service Carvel package from Broadcom; set {\b {\f1 harborServiceYamlFileName}} (with {\b {\f1 parentDirectory}}) under {\f1 supervisorServices}, or use legacy {\b {\f1 harborServiceYamlPath}} / {\b {\f1 harborDataTemplateYamlPath}}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Order and rollback:} Harbor installs after Argo CD. Failure rolls back Harbor only so you can fix and re-run. {\f1 -CleanUp Harbor} removes Harbor without touching Supervisor or Argo CD.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Network segment names} must be lower-case and RFC1123-compliant; they are matched to supervisor.json network references (case-sensitive).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Required vs optional:} In the tables below, {\b Req} reflects whether you typically must supply a value for your environment. {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -ValidateOnly}} (shallow validation) still requires certain {\b keys to exist} in JSON even when defaults apply \u8212- for the authoritative {\b Key / Required / Notes} list (aligned with {\b {\f1 Templates/*-config-help.json}}), run {\b {\f1 Show-InfrastructureJsonConfigurationHelp}} / {\b {\f1 Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp}} or open the copies under {\b {\f1 Docs/}} after {\b {\f1 -Initialize}}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 common\par}
