{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Configuration Help}: Run {\f1 Show-SupervisorJsonConfigurationHelp} to view the full reference (Key, Required, Notes). Use {\f1 -Format List} for narrow screens or {\f1 -Format Table} for wide; {\f1 -Filter} for wildcard search on keys.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Harbor and Argo CD Deployment Notes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harbor: Secret Management and Just-in-Time Environment Variables\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Harbor requires six secrets. Each {\f1 harborConfiguration} secret field supports two modes:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Plain-text in JSON} \u8211- Value is used as-is. Not recommended for production secrets in version-controlled files.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\f1 $env:VARNAME} reference} \u8211- Resolved from the named environment variable at runtime. If the variable is not set when the script runs, you are prompted interactively (masked input) during the pre-flight phase \u8212- before any deployment step begins. The entered value is stored in the process environment for the remainder of the run so each variable is prompted for at most once.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Setting environment variables on Windows before running (recommended for automation):}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 $env:HARBOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD = "MyAdminPassword1"\line
$env:SECRET_KEY             = "MySecretKey12345"   # Must be exactly 16 characters (AES-128)\line
$env:DATABASE_PASSWORD      = "MyDatabasePw1"\line
$env:CORE_SECRET_KEY        = "MyCoreSecret1"\line
$env:JOBSERVICE_SECRET_KEY  = "MyJobSvcSecret1"\line
$env:REGISTRY_SECRET_KEY    = "MyRegSecret1"\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -EdgeSite "site1"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b If env vars are not pre-set,} the script prompts at the start of the run (before connecting to vCenter):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 [WARNING] Environment variable "HARBOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD" (harborConfiguration.harborAdminPassword)\line
          is not set. Prompting for interactive input.\line
Enter value for harborConfiguration.harborAdminPassword (env:HARBOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD): ****\line
\line
[WARNING] Environment variable "SECRET_KEY" (harborConfiguration.secretKey) is not set.\line
          Prompting for interactive input.\line
Enter value for harborConfiguration.secretKey (env:SECRET_KEY): ****************\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b {\f1 secretKey} length:} Must be exactly {\b 16 characters} (AES-128). This constraint is enforced at the point of entry \u8212- whether the value came from a pre-set env var or interactive input:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Pre-set env var with wrong length:} An error is logged, the variable is cleared, and you are prompted to enter a corrected 16-character value before deployment proceeds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Interactive input with wrong length:} The error is shown immediately and the prompt repeats until a valid 16-character value is entered (or Ctrl+C to abort).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Plain-text value in JSON with wrong length:} Caught at pre-flight validation ({\f1 -ValidateOnly} or at the start of any run) before any deployment begins.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example session when {\f1 SECRET_KEY} is set to an 8-character value:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 [ERROR] Environment variable "SECRET_KEY" (harborConfiguration.secretKey) has 8 character(s)\line
        but must be exactly 16.\line
Would you like to re-enter harborConfiguration.secretKey? (Y/N): Y\line
[WARNING] Prompting for corrected value for harborConfiguration.secretKey (env:SECRET_KEY).\line
Enter value for harborConfiguration.secretKey (env:SECRET_KEY): ________\line
[ERROR] harborConfiguration.secretKey must be exactly 16 character(s) but the entered value\line
        is 8 character(s).\line
Would you like to re-enter harborConfiguration.secretKey? (Y/N): Y\line
Enter value for harborConfiguration.secretKey (env:SECRET_KEY): ________________\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Choosing {\b N} at the {\f1 (Y/N)} prompt aborts the deployment cleanly before any vCenter connection or deployment step:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Would you like to re-enter harborConfiguration.secretKey? (Y/N): N\line
[ERROR] User chose not to re-enter harborConfiguration.secretKey. Aborting deployment.\line
Exception: Deployment aborted: harborConfiguration.secretKey must be exactly 16 character(s).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b TLS certificate and private key validation:} {\f1 tlsCrt} and {\f1 caCrt} must contain PEM certificates ({\f1 -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----}). {\f1 tlsKey} must contain a PEM private key. The script validates the first line of each file before deployment and produces a clear error if the types are swapped (a common mistake when the {\f1 .crt} and {\f1 .key} file paths are accidentally transposed).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Debug log redaction:} When running with {\f1 -LogLevel DEBUG}, all secret values in the YAML dump are replaced with {\f1 [REDACTED]}. The private key PEM block ({\f1 tls.key}) is redacted line-by-line. The TLS certificate ({\f1 tls.crt}) is left visible as it is public material. The key name and YAML structure remain intact for structural debugging.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Saving the Harbor data values file ({\f1 -SaveHarborYaml}):} By default, the temporary Harbor data values YAML is deleted after successful installation. Specify {\f1 -SaveHarborYaml} to move it to a {\f1 HarborYaml} subdirectory under the module directory instead:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -SaveHarborYaml\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The directory is created automatically. If it cannot be created, deployment exits with an error before Harbor installation begins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\b Security warning:} The saved file contains {\b Harbor passwords and secrets in plain text}. A {\f1 [WARNING]} is logged whenever this switch is used. A redacted copy is available in the deployment log. Treat the {\f1 HarborYaml} directory like a credentials store \u8212- restrict access, exclude it from source control, and {\b delete the file immediately} after resolving the issue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Harbor credentials display:} After a successful Harbor deployment, the script always prints the Harbor login details (URL, admin username, and password) directly to the console, regardless of the configured {\f1 LogLevel}. This ensures credentials are never silently swallowed even when running at a high log level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Supervisor container image registry registration:} After Harbor succeeds, the module registers it on the Supervisor ({\b Supervisor \u8594 ? Configure \u8594 ? Container Registries} in vCenter) using the Harbor VIP, admin login, and optional {\f1 caCrt}, and sets it as the default registry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On {\f1 -CleanUp Harbor}, {\f1 -CleanUp Supervisor}, or {\f1 -CleanUp All}, that registration is removed before the Harbor service uninstall. Failures there log as warnings; finish the step in vCenter if needed.\par}
