{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All three must be true. If {\f1 labenvironment} is {\f1 false}, or if either {\f1 tlsCrt} or {\f1 tlsKey} is provided (even without the other), auto-generation does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b What the module generates:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab A 2048-bit RSA private key and a self-signed X.509 certificate using .NET cryptography (no OpenSSL dependency). The certificate is valid for 365 days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab The subject CN is set to the effective Harbor hostname ({\f1 harborConfiguration.hostname} from JSON, or the {\f1 hostname:} line from the Harbor data values template when the JSON hostname is also omitted).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Both the certificate and the private key are written as temporary PEM files under the system temp directory for the duration of the deployment, then deleted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab The same certificate is used as the CA certificate ({\f1 ca.crt}) when registering Harbor as a container image registry on the Supervisor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab After a successful deployment, the console notes that TLS is self-signed for lab. Browsers and clients will warn until you trust the certificate or replace it with your own PEMs.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Preserving the generated key and certificate:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Set {\f1 common.preserveAutoGeneratedKeyCertPair: true} to save the generated files after a successful deployment. The module writes them to:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 $env:VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory/HarborKeyCerts/<edgeSite>/<edgeSite>.key\line
$env:VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory/HarborKeyCerts/<edgeSite>/<edgeSite>.crt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On macOS and Linux, the private key is restricted to owner-read-only ({\f1 chmod 0600}). This setting is ignored when {\f1 labenvironment} is {\f1 false} or when you supply your own TLS files.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b What disables auto-generation:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 common.labenvironment: false} \u8212- always uses customer-supplied TLS (or omit the key if disabling Harbor entirely).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Setting {\f1 tlsCrt} {\b or} {\f1 tlsKey} to a non-empty value \u8212- both must be present together; omitting only one is a validation error regardless of lab mode.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Setting {\f1 supervisorServices.disableHarbor: true} \u8212- Harbor is not deployed; TLS is irrelevant.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Important\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Auto-generated self-signed certificates are suitable {\b for lab and demo environments only}. For production, provide your own CA-signed {\f1 tlsCrt} and {\f1 tlsKey} (with optional {\f1 caCrt}) and keep {\f1 common.labenvironment: false}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Argo CD: Deployment Output, Namespace Uniqueness, and Fallback\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Deployment output:} After a successful Argo CD instance deployment, the script logs the initial admin credentials and provides the command to change them:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 [INFO] Login as user "admin" using temporary password: vEHJgoj3wGtHQDlI\line
[INFO] To update your password run: "argocd.exe account update-password\line
       --server 10.40.12.203 --account admin --insecure"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Change the password immediately after deployment using the provided command.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Namespace uniqueness:} The Argo CD namespace in {\f1 argocd-deployment.yml} is automatically suffixed with the cluster MoRef (e.g. {\f1 argocd} \u8594 ? {\f1 argocd-c462}). This prevents conflicts when multiple supervisor clusters share the same vCenter. The base namespace in {\f1 argocd-deployment.yml} must match {\f1 supervisorServices.nameSpacePrefix} in {\f1 infrastructure.json}; the suffix is appended automatically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Argo CD rollback and retry:} If Argo CD deployment fails (pods do not reach ready state, instance timeout, or YAML misconfiguration), rollback removes only the Argo CD namespace. The supervisor and Harbor remain intact. To fix the YAML and retry:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # 1. Remove only the Argo CD namespace; supervisor and Harbor remain intact.\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp ArgoCD -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# 2. Fix your argocd-deployment.yml, then re-run.\line
#    Supervisor and Harbor steps are idempotently skipped.\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -EdgeSite "site1"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Harbor rollback and retry:} Harbor failure rolls back only the Harbor Supervisor Service. The supervisor and Argo CD remain intact and are idempotently verified (not re-deployed) on retry:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # 1. If you declined the rollback prompt or the service is stuck in ERROR state:\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp Harbor -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# 2. Fix the issue (see Troubleshooting below), then retry.\line
#    Supervisor and Argo CD steps are idempotently skipped.\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -EdgeSite "site1"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Autonomous (unattended) runs:} Use {\f1 -RollbackOnFailure $true} to roll back automatically on any failure without prompting (recommended default for unattended pipelines). Use {\f1 -RollbackOnFailure $false} only when you accept a partial deployment and will run scoped {\f1 -CleanUp} yourself before the next attempt. Omit the parameter for an interactive Y/N/Always prompt at each failure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Logging\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The module provides comprehensive logging with multiple levels:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b DEBUG}: Per-step and per-host sub-operation detail (pNIC adds, vmk0 migration, vSAN witness traffic idempotency skips, supervisor build sub-steps, REST session steps, Argo CD per-pod status, etc.). Use {\f1 -LogLevel DEBUG} for troubleshooting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b INFO}: Phase-level milestones visible during a standard deployment (cluster created, hosts added, VDS created, supervisor ready, Argo CD pods ready, Harbor configured, etc.). Default level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b ADVISORY}: Important notices that don't indicate problems.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b WARNING}: Warning messages about potential issues.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b EXCEPTION}: Caught exceptions that were handled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b ERROR}: Error messages indicating failures.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All levels are always written to the log file regardless of the configured {\f1 -LogLevel}. Console output is filtered to the configured level and above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Log files are created under {\b {\f1 Join-Path $env:VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory 'Logs'}} (not under the module folder) with the naming pattern:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 <your-base-directory>/Logs/VcfEdgeAtScale-YYYY-MM-DD.log\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Troubleshooting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Template files or Templates directory missing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -Initialize}} reports missing files under {\b {\f1 Templates}}:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Check module installation path\line
(Get-Module -Name VcfEdgeAtScale -ListAvailable).ModuleBase\line
\line
# Verify Templates exists\line
Test-Path (Join-Path (Get-Module VcfEdgeAtScale -ListAvailable | Select-Object -First 1).ModuleBase "Templates")\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Module Not Found\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the module is not recognized:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Check if module is installed\line
Get-Module -Name VcfEdgeAtScale -ListAvailable\line
\line
# Check module path\line
$env:PSModulePath\line
\line
# Reinstall if needed\line
Install-Module -Name VcfEdgeAtScale -Force\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deployment Failures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\b Enable DEBUG logging}: {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -LogLevel DEBUG}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab {\b Check log files}: Review {\b {\f1 Join-Path $env:VcfEdgeAtScaleRootDirectory 'Logs/VcfEdgeAtScale-*.log'}} (or the path shown in errors)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab {\b Validate JSON files}: Ensure configuration files are valid JSON\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab {\b Verify prerequisites}: Confirm VCF.PowerCLI 9.0+, kubectl, and vcf CLI are available\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cleanup: Management Restore or VDS Removal Fails\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Cleanup no longer requires {\f1 clusters.networking.temporaryManagementIp}. Management (vmk0) is moved from the VDS to a standard switch programmatically using the same API as vCenter\u8217's {\b Migrate VMkernel Adapter} wizard. If that fails, the script reports the error and instructs you to move vmk0 manually in vCenter, then re-run cleanup.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If cleanup reports "could not remove any pNIC from VDS" (vSphere rolled back to avoid disconnecting the host) or "Port group mgmt is in use":\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab In vCenter go to {\b Host \u8594 ? Configure \u8594 ? Networking \u8594 ? VMkernel adapters} for each host in the cluster.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Edit {\b vmk0} and change its port group from the distributed "mgmt" port group to a standard switch (e.g. create a temporary vSwitch with one pNIC and a Management port group, or use an existing standard switch).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Save so vmk0 is on a standard switch on all hosts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab Re-run cleanup with the same parameters (e.g. {\f1 -EdgeSite site2 -CleanUp Compute -Force}). With management off the VDS, cleanup will skip restore and remove the VDS and cluster.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harbor: secretKey Wrong Length\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When using {\f1 $env:} references:} The script detects a wrong-length value during the pre-flight prompting phase and asks whether you want to re-enter:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 [ERROR] Environment variable "SECRET_KEY" (harborConfiguration.secretKey) has 8 character(s)\line
        but must be exactly 16.\line
Would you like to re-enter harborConfiguration.secretKey? (Y/N): Y\line
[WARNING] Prompting for corrected value for harborConfiguration.secretKey (env:SECRET_KEY).\line
Enter value for harborConfiguration.secretKey (env:SECRET_KEY): ________________\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Y} \u8212- re-prompts. The Y/N question repeats after each wrong-length entry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b N} \u8212- aborts deployment cleanly. No rollback is needed; nothing has been deployed yet.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When using a plain-text value in JSON:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 [ERROR] clusters[].harborConfiguration.secretKey for edgeSite "site1" must be\line
        exactly 16 characters but is 8 character(s). Harbor uses it as an AES-128\line
        encryption key.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Fix:} Update the {\f1 secretKey} value in {\f1 infrastructure.json} to exactly 16 characters, then re-run. No rollback is needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harbor: TLS Certificate and Key Swapped (PEM Type Mismatch)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Error:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 [ERROR] clusters[].harborConfiguration.tlsCrt must contain a PEM certificate\line
        (-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----) but the file at "...\\tls.crt" begins with\line
        "-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----" for edgeSite "site1".\line
        Check that tlsCrt and tlsKey paths are not swapped.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Fix:} Verify that {\f1 tlsCrt} points to the certificate file and {\f1 tlsKey} points to the private key file. This error is caught at pre-flight validation before any deployment begins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harbor: Service Enters ERROR State\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On deployment failure, a redacted copy of the Harbor data values file (with password fields replaced by {\f1 [REDACTED]}) is automatically saved for diagnostics \u8212- see the {\f1 [WARNING]} message in the console output for its path. The original secrets file is deleted immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Harbor enters an {\f1 ERROR} state shortly after installation (within 5\u8211-10 seconds of the first status poll):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\b Check vCenter Events} (Menu \u8594 ? Events) for messages containing {\f1 "Registering harbor.tanzu.vmware.com... already exists"}. If present, this is a vCenter async conflict: delete the service from {\b Menu \u8594 ? Supervisor Management \u8594 ? Services}, then roll back and re-run.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab {\b Check the secretKey length} \u8212- Harbor will fail immediately if {\f1 secretKey} is not exactly 16 characters. Look for {\f1 [ERROR]} messages about secretKey length in the pre-flight output.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab {\b Check the vCenter Events for detailed error text} \u8212- the {\f1 ERROR} message from the script (e.g. {\f1 "OCI Registry"}) identifies the failing component but not the root cause. The full error is in vCenter Events for the supervisor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab {\b Admission or scheduling:} HA admission can block new VMs even when free capacity looks sufficient. For vSAN OSA/ESA, try {\b {\f1 haPolicy}} in {\f1 infrastructure.json} ({\f1 reservationBased}, {\f1 slotBased}, or {\f1 disabled}) or change HA admission in vCenter, then retry.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Recovery:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Roll back Harbor only (supervisor and Argo CD remain).\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -CleanUp Harbor -EdgeSite "site1"\line
\line
# Fix the issue, then retry.\line
Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -EdgeSite "site1"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Common Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b "Templates directory not found"}: Reinstall the module or verify FileList in manifest\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b "Required module template is missing"} (during {\b {\f1 -Initialize}}): Reinstall the module; confirm {\b {\f1 FileList}} in {\b {\f1 VcfEdgeAtScale.psd1}} includes the {\b {\f1 Templates/}} files.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b "Unable to determine module installation path"}: Ensure module is properly installed via {\f1 Install-Module}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 PowerShell Module Dependencies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b VCF.PowerCLI}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi0 \f1 Install-Module -Name VCF.PowerCLI\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 External Tools\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b kubectl}: Required for Argo CD operations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b vcf CLI}: Required for supervisor management operations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b veas-json-generator.py} + {\b veas-ui.html} (Python 3.9+, stdlib only \u8212- no extra packages): Browser-based JSON configuration UI copied to {\b {\f1 Tools/}} by {\b {\f1 Start-VcfEdgeAtScale -Initialize}}. Run {\f1 python3 Tools/veas-json-generator.py} from your base directory \u8212- the server starts on {\f1 http://localhost:8080} (localhost only; no TLS) and opens a browser tab automatically. Key flags: {\f1 --port PORT} (default 8080), {\f1 --base-dir DIR} (override JSON directory), {\f1 --no-browser} (headless/SSH). For remote access use SSH port-forwarding. Validates {\b {\f1 infrastructure.json}} and {\b {\f1 supervisor.json}} against the same rules as the PowerShell module (service-aware LB IP minimums, RFC1123 names, cross-file site matching, cross-site ESX host uniqueness, and more). Supports adding new edge sites and cloning existing sites with auto-generated RFC1123 segment names. Saves both files to the base directory (with automatic timestamped backups) or downloads them as a ZIP. See {\b {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#step-3-customize-json-files"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Step 3
}}}
} for full usage details.\sa180\par}
